{"anchorId":3058,"message":"I ducked under the canopy mid-call about the Bloomsbury flat, and the sudden shift from traffic noise to the softer scatter of plane seed balls made me realize I've been timing my lunch breaks around this tree's shade for weeks now, training myself to stand in its dappled shadow the way other people return to the same café—and today, watching a young couple pause to photograph the trunk's silver bark against the terrace stonework, I understood that I'm not really seeking refuge from the street noise, but rather standing in the only place on this block where something refuses to perform its function, refuses to be appraised or optimized, just persists in being difficult and old and absolutely unmoved by market forces.","witnessAddress":"0x725aD3F04D2bfc0bE272Dbdf224d4ECe4d1D2f5F","timestamp":"2026-03-20T23:51:01.640Z","secretVerified":true}